Expected:** 1 chapter (it's a short story without chapter divisions)\n**Actual:** 1 chapter detected\n\nThe structure detection is correct. \"The Masque of the Red Death\" is a continuous short story without explicit chapter breaks. The tool correctly identified it as a single unit.\n\n**Score: 10/10**\n\nNo issues.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.2 Character Extraction (Weight: 25%)\n\n**Expected Characters:**\n1. Prince Prospero (protagonist) - aliases: \"the duke\", \"the prince\"\n2. The Red Death / The masked figure (antagonist) - aliases: \"the mummer\", \"the figure\", \"the stranger\", \"the intruder\"\n\n**Actual:**\n- 1 character: \"the Prince Prospero\" with alias \"the mummer\"\n\n**CRITICAL ISSUES:**\n\n1. **False merge: \"the mummer\" merged with Prince Prospero** - This is factually WRONG. The mummer is the personification of the Red Death, the ANTAGONIST. Prince Prospero PURSUES the mummer with a dagger. They are SEPARATE entities that CONFRONT each other. This is the central conflict of the story.\n\n2. **Missing character: The Red Death / Masked Figure** - The main antagonist is completely absent from the character list. The following aliases should be grouped together as a separate character:\n   - \"the mummer\"\n   - \"the figure\" / \"the masked figure\"\n   - \"the stranger\"\n   - \"the intruder\"\n   - \"the Red Death\" (personification)\n\n3. **Missing alias for Prince Prospero: \"the duke\"** - The text says \"as might have been expected from the duke's love of the bizarre\"\n\n**Score: 3/10** \n\nThe protagonist exists but the antagonist is completely missing, merged incorrectly with the protagonist. This fundamentally misrepresents the story's conflict.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.3 Character Profiles (Weight: 15%)\n\n**Actual Profile for Prince Prospero:**\n> \"Prince Prospero is a tyrannical and self-indulgent ruler who prioritizes his own pleasure and power over the lives of others. His actions—such as ordering the execution of a masked figure and reacting with rage and cowardice when confronted—reveal a character driven by fear, arrogance, and cruelty, ultimately leading to his own demise.\"\n\n**Assessment:**\n- The profile is reasonably accurate for Prince Prospero\n- The evidence quotes are from the text and appropriate\n- Physical descriptions captured (\"bold and robust man\")\n- Key events captured (demanding execution, rushing with dagger, dying)\n\n**However:**\n- The profile conflates the mummer evidence with Prospero (evidence #5 about \"grave cerements and corpse-like mask\" applies to the mummer, not Prospero)\n- Missing relationship: Prospero is the pursuer of the masked figure\n\n**Score: 5/10**\n\nThe profile exists and has some accuracy, but is contaminated by the false merge with the mummer.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.4 Chapter Summaries (Weight: 20%)\n\n**Summary quality:**\nThe summary is comprehensive and accurate:\n- ✅ Correctly describes the Red Death plague\n- ✅ Prince Prospero's retreat to the abbey\n- ✅ The masked ball in seven colored rooms\n- ✅ The ebony clock's effect\n- ✅ The mysterious figure appearing at midnight\n- ✅ Prospero pursuing with a dagger\n- ✅ Prospero's death\n- ✅ The reveal of the Red Death\n\n**Issues:**\n- The summary correctly recognizes the figure as separate from Prospero (\"Prince Prospero, enraged, pursues the figure through the seven rooms with a dagger\") yet the character extraction failed to do so.\n\n**Score: 9/10**\n\nExcellent summary that captures all key plot points. The summary is useful for a narrator.\n\n---\n\n#### 2.5 Pronunciation Guide (Weight: 10%)\n\n**Good flaggings:**\n- \"Prospero\" (/prɒˈspɪəroʊ/) - correct and useful\n- \"improvisatori\" - Italian word, correctly flagged\n- \"arabesque\" - correctly flagged\n- \"cerements\" - correctly flagged (archaic word)\n- \"habiliments\" - correctly flagged\n- \"out-Heroded\" - correctly flagged (Shakespearean reference)\n- \"Hernani\" - correctly flagged (literary reference)\n\n**Homographs correctly identified:**\n-